"'''Jump'''" is a song by American [[rock music|rock]] band [[Van Halen]]. It was released in December 1983 as the lead single of their sixth studio album, ''[[1984 (Van Halen album)|1984]]''. It is Van Halen's most successful single, reaching number 1 on the US [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]].<ref>Whitburn, Joel. ''The Billboard Book of Top 40 Hits'', 6th ed, Billboard Publications, Inc. 1996. {{ISBN|0-8230-7632-6}}</ref> The song differs from earlier Van Halen songs in that it is driven by a [[electronic keyboard|keyboard]] riff, although the song does contain a [[guitar solo]]. [[David Lee Roth]] dedicated the song to martial artist [[Benny Urquidez|Benny "The Jet" Urquidez]], of whom he was a student.<ref name="Christe2009">{{cite book |last=Christe|first=Ian|author-link=Ian Christe|title=Everybody Wants Some: The Van Halen Saga|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=qyhHmGh_9fEC&pg=PA97|year=2009|publisher=John Wiley & Sons|isbn=978-0-470-53618-6|page=97}}</ref> In 2021, ''[[Rolling Stone]]'' ranked "Jump" at number 177 on its updated list of the "[[Rolling Stone's 500 Greatest Songs of All Time|500 Greatest Songs of All Time]]".<ref>{{cite magazine|last=|first=|date=September 15, 2021|title=Jump ranked #177 on Rolling Stone 500 Greatest Songs List|url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-lists/best-songs-of-all-time-1224767/van-halen-jump-3-1225161/|access-date=September 25, 2021|magazine=Rolling Stone}}</ref>

"Jump" was one of the few Van Halen songs originally recorded by Roth that [[Sammy Hagar]] would perform live during his tenures with the band.

